[EVIDENCE]
UID: EV-TRANSITIONS-ENGINE-SPIKE
TITLE: pytransitions as the semantics engine: findings from the spike
DEPTH: implemented
AUTHORED_BY: llm
SOURCE: dev/scripts/sdoc_semantics/, transitions 0.9.3, dev shell + strictdoc-grammar-extract, 2026-09; see dev/scripts/test_sdoc_semantics.py for the exercised traps
STATEMENT: >>>
A spike on `transitions` (pytransitions) 0.9.3 as the semantics engine behind
DEPTH, DECISION STATUS, and AUTHORED_BY, landed at
dev/scripts/sdoc_semantics/. Three library traps were measured and are all
avoided by construction rather than by an assertion after the fact:
`add_ordered_transitions` ROTATES its sequence to the machine's `initial`,
dropping the first edge for a wrap-around one nobody asked for (avoided by
declare.ordered expanding adjacent pairs itself, and by Semantic.initial being
the first rung by construction); that same helper's `loop=True` default closes
every ladder into a ring (same avoidance -- the shape cannot be produced
here); and `Machine`'s `auto_transitions=True` default synthesises a
`to_<state>()` trigger per state that never appears in `markup` but does
appear in `get_triggers()`, which would silently empty every machine's
`terminal` list since that is exactly the call terminal detection makes
(passed off explicitly as `auto_transitions=False` everywhere a Machine is
built).

Mermaid rendering (`transitions.extensions.diagrams.GraphMachine`,
`graph_engine="mermaid"`) needs no graphviz -- it is a text emitter, not a
renderer -- so `mermaid()` in engine.py has a real fallback path (returning
`None` on any exception, including the extra being absent) rather than one
that can never fire on this build.

What the library cannot express is two shapes, both left to callers: a
cross-node invariant (STATUS-SUPERSEDE-NEEDS-A-TARGET -- reaching `superseded`
without a `Superseded_By` relation is a dangling supersession, which is a rule
about the GRAPH, not a rule about one node's field) and a relation BETWEEN two
nodes' fields (nothing here compares one item's DEPTH against another's).
Neither is a `transitions` gap so much as a scope boundary: the library models
one field's own lifecycle and nothing wider -- no actor attached to a
transition (who may raise AUTHORED_BY is unruled), and no notion of a
transition rippling into a second machine.

Delivered to both interpreters that run this code: `grammarPython` in
devenv.nix (`pkgs.python3.withPackages (ps: [ps.ast-grep-py ps.transitions])`,
the board server's own interpreter) and `strictdocGrammarExtract`
(packages/strictdoc-grammar/lib/mkExtract.nix, the wrapper every scribe
program runs under, which every other strictdoc check in this repo already
uses) -- `transitions` is pure Python with no compiled coupling to the
tree-sitter grammar the extractor pins, so it rides along in both without the
minor-version alignment the compiled extras need.

The three eval-time diagnostics engine.py computes by INTERROGATING the built
machine rather than trusting the declaration: an unreachable state (declared
but no transition reaches it), a lifecycle with no terminal state at all
(`get_triggers(state) == []` empty across every state), and two transitions
firing on the same trigger from the same state (an ambiguous dispatch). All
three are exercised in dev/scripts/test_sdoc_semantics.py by building a
naively-shaped machine first and showing the diagnostic fire on it, then
showing the shipped machines pass clean -- an absence assertion over a shape
that cannot be produced proves nothing on its own.

`scribe semantics` (dev/scripts/scribe_cmd.py) answers with the daemon down: it
imports sdoc_semantics directly rather than going through the RPC socket, so a
missing engine costs one subcommand rather than the whole CLI, and the board
degrades the same way -- the payload's absence yields a named "unavailable"
shape rather than a missing key.
<<<
